Group B: Long Questions 

5. Compare and contrast Hierarchical (Tree) structure and Linear (Sequential) structure. Give an example of when to use each.
ans: A hierarchical (tree) structure organizes website content in a parent-child relationship , where main pages branch into sub pages. it allows users to navigate through categories and subcategories . 

A linear(Sequential) structure organizes contents in a fixed , step by step order where users move form one page to the next . it is simple and easy to follow but limited in navigation options.

Comparison:
i. Hierarchical structure offers flexible navigation , while linear structure follows fixed path.
ii. Hierarchical structure is suitable for large websites , while linear structure is ideal for small or guided processes .
iii. Hierarchical structure is complex in design, while linear structure is easy to understand.
iv. In Hierarchical structure users can quickly locate specific contents , wjile in linear structure users can only follow fixed path.

Examples:
Hierarchical structure: University website(Admissions→ Programs→Departmets).
Linear structure: online tutorial or checkout process.

6. Explain the ”Three-Click Rule” and its significance in User Experience (UX) design.
ans: The "Three-Click Rule" states that users chould be able to find any information on a website within three clicks. if the users takes more than three clicks, they may become frustrated and leave the site.

Significance in UX Design:
i.Improves Nvigation Efficiency 
  •Reduces the time and effort users spend searching for specific information.
ii.Enhances User Satisfaction
  •Users feel the website is easy to use and intuitive. 
  •Leads to higher trust and positive perception towards the website.
iii.Reduce Bounce Rate 
  •If users cannot find what they need quickly, they leave the site.
  •following the three-click rule helps retain visitors.
iv.Supports logical information Architecture
  •Encourages designers to organize content in a clear , hierarchical structure.

  Example:
  Good UX: Homepage→Serves→Web Design→Portfolio
  Bad UX: Hoepage→About us→Services→Web Design→Portfolio

